course: dictionary
lesson: 7806
title: Quechua: a language and a people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Quechua is a family of languages spoken in the Andes mountains of South America, mostly in Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador. It is also the name of the peoples who speak these languages. «Quechua was the language of the Inca Empire.» Here Quechua names a language, because it was the language the Inca Empire spoke.
explain: Look at the words around it to find its job. In «speak Quechua», it names a language. In «Quechua people», it describes which people we mean, like a describing word. Ask yourself: is this naming a language, a group of people, or describing something? The clues around the word tell you.
explain: Quechua is not just history. «Many Indigenous languages are still spoken, such as Quechua by several million people in the Andes, Guaraní in Paraguay and Nahuatl in Mexico.» So millions of people still speak Quechua today.
explain: Some English words came from Quechua, such as llama, condor, puma, jerky and quinine. English borrowed these words, so you may already know some of them without realising where they came from.
fact: Quechua is a family of languages, so it is a group of related languages, not just one.
fact: The Quechua are the peoples who speak these languages.
fact: Quechua was the main language of the Inca Empire.
fact: Quechua is spoken by millions of Indigenous people in the Andes mountains of South America.
fact: English words such as llama, condor, puma, jerky and quinine came from Quechua.
exercise: «Quechua was the language of the Inca Empire.» In this sentence, what does Quechua name? | *A language | A mountain | A country | A bird
exercise: Which country is not one of the main places where Quechua is spoken? | *Australia | Bolivia | Peru | Ecuador
exercise: Which English word came from Quechua? | telephone | bicycle | *condor
test: In «Quechua people», what kind of word is Quechua? | A noun for a mountain | A verb, a doing word | *A describing word, like an adjective
test: Which clue shows that Quechua is still spoken today? | *Several million people still speak Quechua in the Andes | Quechua is a family of languages | Quechua was the language of the Inca Empire
exam: Which answer best describes Quechua? | A kind of metal | *A family of languages spoken in the Andes mountains, and the name of the people who speak them | A large river in Africa | A type of cooking pot
